    description: `
The first direct detection of gravitational waves was a major breakthrough in physics and astronomy. Albert Einstein's general theory of relativity, published in 1915, describes gravity as the curvature of spacetime produced by mass and energy. The theory also predicts that accelerating massive objects can generate ripples in spacetime that travel outward at the speed of light. These ripples are known as gravitational waves.

For decades, gravitational waves were studied mainly through mathematical theory and indirect evidence. The challenge was that by the time a wave reaches Earth, its effect is extraordinarily small. Even powerful astrophysical events, such as two black holes merging, produce changes in distance far smaller than the size of an atomic nucleus across kilometre-scale detectors.

The Laser Interferometer Gravitational-Wave Observatory, or LIGO, was designed to measure these tiny changes. It uses two large interferometers, one in Washington State and one in Louisiana. Each instrument sends laser light along two long, perpendicular arms. Mirrors reflect the light back, and the returning beams interfere with each other. A passing gravitational wave slightly changes the relative lengths of the arms, altering the interference pattern.

On September 14, 2015, during an engineering observation period associated with Advanced LIGO, both observatories detected a signal later identified as GW150914. The signal had the characteristic pattern expected from two black holes spiralling toward each other, merging, and settling into a single remnant black hole. The event was announced publicly in February 2016 after extensive analysis and checks.

The signal revealed that the black holes had masses of several tens of times the Sun's mass. As they approached one another, their orbital speed increased and the gravitational-wave frequency rose. The final merger released a tremendous amount of energy in gravitational waves within a fraction of a second. This energy came from the system's mass, as described by the relationship between mass and energy.

The discovery was important because it created a new way to observe objects that emit little or no light. Traditional astronomy relies largely on electromagnetic radiation, including visible light, radio waves, infrared, ultraviolet, X-rays, and gamma rays. Gravitational-wave detectors instead measure changes in spacetime itself. They can reveal mergers of black holes that may be difficult or impossible to observe with conventional telescopes.

Subsequent detections have included merging black holes and neutron stars. In August 2017, gravitational waves from a binary neutron-star merger were detected alongside electromagnetic signals observed by telescopes. That event connected gravitational-wave astronomy with traditional astronomy and provided evidence about the origin of many heavy elements, including some forms of gold and platinum, through neutron-star merger processes.

Gravitational-wave astronomy also tests general relativity in extreme environments and helps scientists estimate distances to distant events. Future observatories on Earth and in space may detect lower-frequency waves from supermassive black-hole systems and other sources. The first detection demonstrated that the universe can be studied through more than light alone, creating an entirely new observational field and allowing scientists to investigate some of the most energetic events in cosmic history.
`
